# CAMPAIGN STATE IS NOT SOURCE. Everything in discovery_okuda/campaign/ except the four inputs
# below is written by a running loop and DELETED by campaign_loop.clean_start() at every launch --
# so committing it buys nothing and costs the one thing that actually hurt: `git stash` and
# `git restore` will happily rewrite a live campaign's registry underneath the process using it.
# That happened twice on 4 August. The second time, a stash taken while a campaign was running
# rolled hypotheses.jsonl back to a dead campaign's copy; round 2 then posed a hypothesis id that
# copy already held and died with `R2.0.f4907e already posed`, and the live round's own records
# went with the dropped stash.
#
# The research record is elsewhere and stays tracked: discovery_okuda/_archive/, the _failed_*
# snapshots, and the note. What is kept here is only what a fresh clone NEEDS to run a campaign --
# the two templates, the standing instructions, and the human's channel -- which are exactly the
# files clean_start() preserves by name.
discovery_okuda/campaign/*
!discovery_okuda/campaign/TEMPLATE_analysis.md
!discovery_okuda/campaign/TEMPLATE_memory.md
!discovery_okuda/campaign/instruction.md
# THE CLAIM LEDGER IS TRACKED, and the reason is that it was already lost once. `clean_start()`
# deleted it on the claim campaign's first launch -- `claims.jsonl` was on CAMPAIGN_STATE for an
# older file of the same name -- and the only copy was a scratch backup in /tmp that happened to
# still exist. It is not campaign state and it is not a snapshot: it is what the campaign KNOWS,
# it is hand-seeded, and a fresh clone needs it to run a claim campaign at all. Tracked, so the
# next time something deletes it the recovery is `git checkout` rather than luck.
!discovery_okuda/campaign/claims.jsonl
!discovery_okuda/campaign/user_input.md

# ECM sweep output: movies and strips, regenerated by prototype/ecm/sweep.py
log/okuda_ECM/

# GENERATED PER-ROUND CONFIGS. `translate.write_config` writes one yaml and one composition.json per
# slot per round, so a twelve-slot campaign of twenty rounds is 480 files that are outputs, not inputs
# -- and the authoritative copy of a run's spec is `spec_run.yaml` inside the run's own directory. The
# reference recipes (coral_gate, cfl_*, wk_*, repair_*) stay tracked: those are chosen starting points.
config/okuda/r[0-9][0-9][0-9]_*.yaml
config/okuda/r[0-9][0-9][0-9]_*.composition.json
config/okuda/r[0-9][0-9][0-9]c_*.yaml
config/okuda/r[0-9][0-9][0-9]c_*.composition.json
config/okuda/r[0-9][0-9][0-9]n_*.yaml
config/okuda/r[0-9][0-9][0-9]n_*.composition.json
config/okuda/demo_*.yaml
config/okuda/demo_*.composition.json
config/okuda/tzz_*.yaml
config/okuda/tzz_*.composition.json

# The JEPA feasibility scratch is GONE, not ignored: `discovery_okuda/surrogate.py` rebuilds its
# design matrix from the run directories on every call, so a 2.9 GB cache of embeddings, model
# weights and one-off agent scripts was a second copy of something derivable -- and 32 of its files
# (HuggingFace lock files among them) had been committed. The pattern stays so a stray re-run does
# not re-commit them.
discovery_okuda/jepa/

# LaTeX build artefacts beside the notes -- the .tex and .pdf are tracked, the rest is regenerable
discovery_okuda/*.aux
discovery_okuda/*.log
discovery_okuda/*.out
discovery_okuda/*.fls
discovery_okuda/*.fdb_latexmk
